        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should granite countertops be sealed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Test annually and seal only when the test fails. In practice a quality impregnator lasts three to five years on mid-density granite, two to three on coarse light stone or honed finishes, and dense black granite frequently never needs it. The once-a-year rule dates from older sealer chemistry."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you seal granite too often?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Sealer that cannot penetrate dries on the surface as a slightly tacky film that attracts dirt and dulls the polish, and repeated unnecessary applications build up a haze in the least-cleaned areas. Testing first avoids it entirely."
          }
        },
