            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is cleaned most, it has standing water on it most, and detergents are what gradually remove an impregnating sealer. That combination means the sink run typically fails the water test two or three years before the island does, which is why spot sealing it makes sense."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does more expensive granite sealer last longer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually. Fluoropolymer impregnators, particularly solvent-carried ones, give three to five years on mid-density granite where a budget water-based silicone gives one to two. A bottle covers several kitchens, so the price difference is small against the afternoons it saves."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I reseal before selling the house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the water test fails. A hazy over-sealed countertop looks worse to a buyer than a clean unsealed one, and sealing changes nothing visually when it is applied correctly. Removing any existing stains matters far more to how the kitchen presents."
          }
