    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealing granite annually because a fabricator said so is like changing the oil every thousand miles. It is not harmful, exactly, but it is work you did not need to do and it tells you nothing about the state of the stone.
        </p>

        <div id="rule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Where the Annual Rule Came From
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seal your granite once a year is advice that predates modern impregnating sealers by a couple of decades. The products available when granite countertops became common in domestic kitchens were largely silicone and siloxane based, they sat close to the surface, and cleaning genuinely did wear them off within a year or so. For those products the advice was sound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Current fluoropolymer impregnators behave differently. They deposit a repellent deep in the pore structure and bond there, and on a normally absorbent granite a properly applied two-coat application lasts three to five years. The advice never updated, partly through inertia and partly because the people repeating it most often are the people selling sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule also ignores the enormous variation between slabs. A dense black granite may be effectively non-porous and need sealing never; a coarse white may need it every two years. Applying a single interval across all granite guarantees that most people are either doing unnecessary work or, worse, doing nothing while relying on a schedule that does not fit their stone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest replacement is a test rather than a date. Fifteen minutes with a cup of water tells you the actual state of the countertop in front of you, and it costs nothing. Our guide to <a href='/how-to-tell-if-granite-needs-sealing/'>telling whether granite needs sealing</a> sets it out step by step.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a commercial angle worth naming. Sealer is a consumable, and the interval at which it is recommended is set by people who benefit from a shorter one. That does not make the advice dishonest — fabricators genuinely do see stained countertops and would rather you over-maintained than under-maintained — but it does mean the number you are given is conservative by design, and treating it as a physical property of granite is a mistake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depends" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Decides the Interval
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four things govern how long a sealing job lasts, and none of them is the calendar. The first is the stone's own porosity, which varies by an order of magnitude between a dense black and an open-grained white. The second is the sealer's chemistry and quality. The third is how it was applied, specifically whether it was given a proper dwell and two coats. The fourth is how the countertop is cleaned.
          </p>

          <p>
            Of those, cleaning is the one most people control without realising it. Impregnating sealers are gradually removed by repeated exposure to detergents, and by anything alkaline or acidic. A countertop wiped daily with a general-purpose kitchen spray loses its sealer substantially faster than one cleaned with warm water and a stone-safe product, on the same stone with the same sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Application quality accounts for most of the rest. A single coat wiped on and off in two minutes provides perhaps a year of protection; the same product applied in two coats with a full dwell can last four. That is a difference created entirely on the afternoon of the job, and it is the reason our guide to <a href='/how-to-seal-granite-countertops/'>sealing granite countertops</a> is so insistent about the dwell time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish is the quiet fourth factor. Honed and leathered granite has a more open surface than polished, and everything above applies more aggressively to it. If you have a honed countertop, assume the shorter end of every interval in this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water hardness plays a smaller role that still shows up over years. In hard water areas, mineral deposits build around the tap and get scrubbed at more aggressively, and it is that repeated scrubbing rather than the minerals themselves that wears the sealer through. A countertop in a hard water region typically loses its protection around the sink noticeably faster than the same installation elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Realistic Intervals by Stone and Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the intervals we would plan around, on the understanding that the test always overrides the table. Treat the figures as when to start testing seriously rather than as when to seal.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stone</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Quality Impregnator</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Budget Sealer</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Test From</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dense black / dark green</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often never needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often never needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Year 3</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mid-density brown / grey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Year 2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Light speckled granite</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Year 2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coarse white / Kashmir</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Annually</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Year 1</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Honed or leathered finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Annually</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Year 1</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The budget column is the honest reason the annual rule persists. Cheap water-based silicone sealers really do need redoing every year or two on absorbent stone, so someone using one is not wrong to be sealing annually — they are just paying in weekends for what they saved on the bottle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice that the dark stone rows say never rather than giving a number. That is not an oversight. A significant share of granite countertops cannot absorb a sealer at all, and for those the correct maintenance schedule is to test every few years and otherwise leave the stone alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            One interval that is not on the table but should be in your calendar: test after any building work, refinishing or professional deep clean. Aggressive cleaning products and stone polishing both strip sealer, and a countertop that passed last year can fail immediately after a kitchen refurbishment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="habits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Habits That Shorten the Interval
          </h2>

          <p>
            Daily cleaning with the wrong product is the biggest one. General-purpose sprays, anything containing ammonia, degreasers and citrus or vinegar cleaners all attack impregnating sealers. Warm water with a few drops of washing-up liquid, or a dedicated stone cleaner, does the same cleaning job without the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standing water is the second. A countertop that regularly has puddles around the tap and the drainer is being asked to resist absorption far more often than one that gets wiped dry. It is the reason the area beside the sink almost always fails the water test a year or more before the island does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot pans placed directly on the stone are a third, and one people rarely connect to sealing. Granite tolerates heat well, but the sealer in the top layer of pore structure does not, and a repeated hot spot under the same trivet-free pan will lose its protection first. Use a trivet — not to protect the granite, but to protect the sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, oil. Cooking oil left standing on granite is the hardest thing an impregnator has to deal with, and the area around the cooktop is where sealer gives up first in most kitchens. Wiping the oil ring off the countertop each evening is worth a year of interval on its own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="over" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Cost of Over-Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing granite that does not need it is not neutral. An impregnator that cannot enter the stone has nowhere to go, so it dries on the surface as a thin, uneven film. That film is slightly tacky, it attracts dirt, it dulls the polish in patches, and it has to be re-dissolved and buffed off with the same solvent it arrived in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repeated over-sealing compounds it. Each unnecessary application adds a little more residue than the last buff removed, and after three or four annual applications on a dense stone the countertop can look permanently slightly hazy in exactly the areas that get least cleaning — under the fruit bowl, behind the tap, along the backsplash.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE THAT REPLACES THE CALENDAR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Test annually. Seal only when the test fails.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Fifteen minutes with a cup of water, three spots, once a year. If nothing darkens, the sealer is doing its job and adding more achieves nothing but residue. If it darkens, you have caught it early enough that a single maintenance coat is all it takes.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is a cost argument too, though it is the smaller one. A bottle of quality impregnator is 25 to 60 dollars and covers several kitchens' worth of countertop, so the money is not really the issue — the afternoon is. Testing takes fifteen minutes and sealing takes an afternoon plus a day of curing, and doing the second one when the first would have said no is the actual waste.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of which is an argument for neglect. Under-sealing an absorbent granite costs far more than over-sealing a dense one, because absorbed oil stains are difficult to remove and occasionally permanent. The test settles both risks at once, which is why it is the whole recommendation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Spot Sealing the Areas That Fail First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Countertops do not wear evenly, and there is no rule that says the whole surface must be resealed at once. The strip beside the sink, the area around the cooktop and the section by the kettle typically fail the water test two or three years before the island and the back runs. Treating those areas on their own is entirely legitimate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The method is identical to a full seal, just applied to the failing section: clean, dry thoroughly, apply generously, keep wet for the full dwell, buff off completely. Feather the edge of the treated area by a few inches into stone that passed, so there is no hard line between sealed and unsealed.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no visible difference between spot-sealed and fully sealed granite, because a correctly applied impregnator changes nothing about how the stone looks. The exception is colour-enhancing sealers, which do change the appearance and therefore must never be spot applied — those are a whole-countertop commitment every time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spot sealing every couple of years around the sink, with a full application every four or five, is the maintenance pattern that keeps a kitchen protected for the least work. It also concentrates the effort where the risk is, which is where the water and the oil land.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Maintenance Plan Worth Following
          </h2>

          <p>
            Set one annual reminder, in a month you will not resent it — early autumn works well, since the countertop is not needed for a summer gathering and the kitchen gets less traffic. Run the water test on three fixed spots: beside the sink, next to the cooktop, and in the middle of the island.
          </p>

          <p>
            If all three pass, do nothing and note the date. If one fails, spot seal that area. If two or three fail, do the whole countertop in two coats. That is the entire decision tree, and it replaces every rule of thumb you will read about granite sealing frequency.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the sealer bottle with the date of the last application written on it. Knowing what product went on and when makes the next job trivial to plan, and it means a maintenance coat is always the same product rather than an unknown one layered over an unknown one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, protect the sealer between applications: stone-safe cleaner, trivets under hot pans, chopping boards rather than the stone itself, and wiping standing water and oil rather than leaving them. Good habits between seals extend the interval further than any upgrade in product does, and they are free. Our <a href='/best-granite-sealer/'>tested sealer picks</a> are worth the money, but they cannot outperform a countertop that gets sprayed with degreaser every evening.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the test before you buy anything, not after. The natural sequence is to notice a mark, order sealer, and apply it the day it arrives — which skips the step that tells you whether any of that was necessary and which areas need it. Fifteen minutes with a glass of water first turns the whole job into a decision rather than a reflex.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Annually Out of Habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The yearly rule dates from older silicone sealers. A quality fluoropolymer impregnator applied in two coats lasts three to five years on most granite, and a dense black stone may never need sealing at all. Test rather than schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Stone That Cannot Absorb It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Product that cannot enter the stone dries on top as a tacky, dirt-attracting film that dulls the polish in patches. Repeated over several years it leaves a permanent-looking haze in the areas that get cleaned least.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning With General-Purpose Spray</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ammonia, degreasers, citrus and vinegar cleaners all strip impregnating sealer. Warm water with a little washing-up liquid, or a dedicated stone cleaner, cleans just as well and can double the interval between seals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing the Whole Countertop Every Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wear concentrates beside the sink and around the cooktop. Spot sealing those areas as they fail, with a full application only every four or five years, is less work and puts the product where the risk actually is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Test After Building Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Refinishing, deep cleaning and construction dust all strip or contaminate sealer. A countertop that passed last year can fail the week after a kitchen refurbishment, so test again once the work is done rather than waiting for the annual reminder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should granite countertops be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test annually and seal only when the test fails. In practice a quality impregnator lasts three to five years on mid-density granite, two to three on coarse light stone or honed finishes, and dense black granite frequently never needs it. The once-a-year rule dates from older sealer chemistry.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal granite too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sealer that cannot penetrate dries on the surface as a slightly tacky film that attracts dirt and dulls the polish, and repeated unnecessary applications build up a haze in the least-cleaned areas. Testing first avoids it entirely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when it is time to reseal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pour a quarter cup of water on a clean, dry, well-used area and leave it fifteen minutes. If wiping it away leaves a dark patch that takes time to fade, that area needs sealing. Test three fixed spots annually: beside the sink, near the cooktop, and on the island.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealed granite need resealing after deep cleaning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Professional deep cleans, refinishing and aggressive degreasers all strip impregnating sealer. Run the water test once the work is finished rather than waiting for your usual annual check, because the countertop can go from protected to open in a single afternoon.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the area by the sink need sealing first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is cleaned most, it has standing water on it most, and detergents are what gradually remove an impregnating sealer. That combination means the sink run typically fails the water test two or three years before the island does, which is why spot sealing it makes sense.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does more expensive granite sealer last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually. Fluoropolymer impregnators, particularly solvent-carried ones, give three to five years on mid-density granite where a budget water-based silicone gives one to two. A bottle covers several kitchens, so the price difference is small against the afternoons it saves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I reseal before selling the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the water test fails. A hazy over-sealed countertop looks worse to a buyer than a clean unsealed one, and sealing changes nothing visually when it is applied correctly. Removing any existing stains matters far more to how the kitchen presents.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

